<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ban Kulin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1180–<a href="/years/1204">1204</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Kulin of Bosnia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulin">Kulin</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> from about 1180 to 1204, nominally under Hungarian and Byzantine overlordship but in practice independently, and his reign was later remembered as a golden age. His charter of 1189 granting free trade to the merchants of Ragusa is one of the oldest state documents in a South Slavic language. Accused of protecting heretics, he and his nobles publicly abjured heresy at Bilino Polje in 1203.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ban-kulin.jpg" alt="Ban Kulin" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plo%C4%8Da_Kulina_bana_iz_crkve_kod_Visokog.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
